A||N||4||pherder||WOOHOO!!!  The Clutch works!!!!!!!||||10||
Z||000000||pherder||08-27-2000||03:13 PM||||After a lot of work and the talents of Rodney Dickman, I have a working clutch in the 88 Coupe with the 93 HO Quad 4 and the 97 Isuzu 5 speed.<P>Let my "fun" be a lesson to all who attempt an engine swap without using pieces from ONE donor!!!!!<P>Rodney supplied an adapter so that I could bolt on a stock Fiero's Getrag/Muncie slave cylinder on to a transaxle that has an external slave BUT an internal release arm.<P>I finally got everything attached, the engine running with NO codes!  And now had to get the clutch working.  First I removed the slave that came with the transaxle.  It is huge!!  The Fiero's slave will NOT push enough fluid thru it to make it work.  Since I modified the stock clutch line to work with the supplied slave (i.e. I cut off the end of the flex tube and pushed it on the slave's barbed end and clamped it on), I had to replace the line.  Be advised, gmpartsdirect.com shows it under part number 14105670 for ~ $60.<P>I now have a new master, slave, and line.<P>After using Archie's method of bleeding the line, I still had air.  One problem with using his instructions with my slave, my slave must be removed from the transaxle to do the final bleed and I think that sucks in more air.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">  One other problem would be that this was a brand new clutch line with factory air installed!  ;^)  I probably didn't have all the air out in the two attemps in bleeding it.  (i.e. push the clutch pedal down, open bleed screw, watch air/fluid come out, close bleed screw, release pedal, check master cylinder for level ... 5 times each time!!!)<P>I attempted his method twice and then tried someone's elses way and ended up just taking out the bleed screw and letting the fluid drain from the master to the slave.  It worked!  Got more air out that way and finally ended up with just fluid running out.  I replaced the bleed screw and tried the clutch ... Good strong clutch action with a release point about half way (or higher).  Started the engine and put the transaxle in 1st and reverse and both worked beautifully!!!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Since it is the 18 or 19th day of over 100 degrees here this August, I am in cooling off.  The shop's A/C just doesn't keep up very well.  Got to get the shop insulated.<P>Paul<BR>88 Coupe w/HO Quad 4<BR>88 Formula w/leaky HO 3.1<BR>88 GT w/o engine at this time.||||reg||10
Z||000001||Cooter||08-27-2000||03:40 PM||||Wow! You have air conditioning in your shop? Hey buddy, I need a little work done. Can I come over for a few weeks?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>The slower they think you are, the more suprised they will be when you smoke them.||||reg||1
Z||000002||pherder||08-27-2000||04:01 PM||||Hey Cooter!<P>Hmmmm?  I have three 88 Fieros that I am working on in a 2 car garage.  Don't think I have the room for more.  I know, just ask the wife ... I am dead meat IF I try to bring another ANYTHING home.<P>You anywhere near Birmingham?  I will be there at the airport taking down the servers at the Raytheon Aircraft Services site.  Seems the site was sold to another company but not the servers so I get to shut them down and get them shipped back to Wichita, Kansas.<P>Will be there Thursday @ noon and leaving Friday @ 6 PM.<P>Paul<BR>||||reg||1
